(window.LADDER_CARDS["25·eng"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["25·eng"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"25·eng.1.1","q":"Why is a force a number of the plane, and what is steady along a hanging chain?","a":"a force has a size and a direction, so it is across part plus i times up part; the weight has no across part, so along the chain the across part of the pull is the same everywhere, and the up part is that across part times the slope","w":["a force is a real number; the whole pull is the same everywhere along the chain","a force is a number of the plane; the up part of the pull is the same everywhere","a force has no direction; nothing is steady along the chain"],"why":"Split each force along directions chosen so that the loads touch as few parts as possible, and say which you chose."},
 {"u":"25·eng.2.1","q":"What shape does a chain take under its own weight, and why is every hanging chain one curve?","a":"y = c cosh(x/c), with c its lowest pull over its weight per metre; it is the curve cosh stretched by c, the map z ↦ cz, so every hanging chain is one curve, stretched and slid","w":["a parabola y = x²/(2c); every chain is one parabola","y = c cosh(x/c); chains of different c are different curves, not stretches of one","an arc of a circle of radius c"],"why":"A 300 m span with c = 1000 m sags about 11.27 m."},
 {"u":"25·eng.3.1","q":"What does a hanging chain become turned upside down, and which flip of the forces makes that work?","a":"an arch that stands under the same loads by pushing alone; flip every point across the floor, z ↦ z̄, and send each force F to −F̄: the flip of a sum is the sum of the flips, so each joint still balances, the weights still point down, and every pull is now a push","w":["an arch that pulls; flip the forces by F ↦ F̄","an arch that stands only without loads; flip the forces by F ↦ −F","a chain again; no flip is needed"],"why":"Stone can push and cannot pull."},
 {"u":"25·eng.4.1","q":"What shape is a cable carrying a level deck, and why is it a parabola when a chain under its own weight is not?","a":"a parabola, with across pull qL²/(8s): the flatter the cable, the harder it pulls; the deck's load is even across the span, so Hy″ = q, while a chain's weight is even along the chain, more per metre across where it is steep","w":["a cosh curve; the deck's load is even along the cable","a parabola; a chain is one too, to every place","a straight line between the towers"],"why":"cosh u = 1 + u²/2 + ⋯: a flat chain is nearly a parabola, and not one."},
 {"u":"25·eng.5.1","q":"What decides whether an arch stands, and why does the squeeze on the stone grow only in proportion to the size?","a":"its shape, not its size, until it is so large that its stone is crushed; stretch the arch by c and every weight and force grows by c³ while the faces they press on grow by c², so the squeeze grows by c","w":["its size: a twice as large arch needs twice as strong stone at every size","its stone alone; the squeeze grows by c³","its shape; the squeeze does not grow at all"],"why":"A granite column crushes its own base only at about 4.9 km tall."},
 {"u":"25·eng.6.1","q":"What is out of place in “a hanging chain is a parabola”, and in “pull a chain hard enough and it hangs straight”?","a":"a chain under its own weight is c cosh(x/c); the parabola is for a load even across; and the sag, about L²/(8c), is never 0 for a finite pull","w":["a chain is an arc of a circle; and a chain hangs straight once H = wL","nothing in the first; in the second the sag doubles with the pull","a chain is a parabola only when long; and the sag is 0 when c = L"],"why":"The curves agree to the first terms only."});
/* end phase F und_r25 */
